menu "Stadiacast dongle"

config DONGLE_SSID
    string "Hotspot SSID"
    default "stadiacast-dongle"
    help
        Network the Stadia controller joins. Provision the controller with this name
        (stadiacast-provision) and use the same name and password for the PC hotspot,
        so the controller works with either.

config DONGLE_PASSWORD
    string "Hotspot password (WPA2, 8 to 63 characters)"
    default "stadiacast"

config DONGLE_CHANNEL
    int "Wi-Fi channel"
    range 1 13
    default 6

config DONGLE_USB_AUDIO
    bool "USB headphones for the controller's headset jack"
    default y
    help
        Besides the gamepad, the board shows up as USB headphones (48 kHz stereo). What the
        computer plays there goes to the controller's 3.5 mm jack as Opus. Turn it off to be a
        gamepad only, as in step 3.

config DONGLE_OPUS_COMPLEXITY
    int "Opus encoder complexity"
    range 1 10
    default 8
    depends on DONGLE_USB_AUDIO
    help
        Higher is better sound for more CPU time per 20 ms frame. The serial log prints the
        encode time every 10 s while audio plays.

config DONGLE_USB_MIC
    bool "Pass the controller's microphone to the USB headset"
    default y
    depends on DONGLE_USB_AUDIO
    help
        Decode the audio the controller sends (Opus over SRTP, on either WebRTC link) and play it
        as the USB headset's microphone (48 kHz mono). The USB microphone is there either way;
        without this it stays silent.

config DONGLE_MIC_REQUEST
    bool "Ask for the microphone in SetAudioMode (experimental)"
    default y
    depends on DONGLE_USB_MIC
    help
        Set the SetAudioModeRequest's f2 flag, whose meaning is not known yet: our guess for
        "send the microphone". If the controller refuses the request with it, the board asks
        again without it, so the headphones keep working.

config DONGLE_PAD_MODE
    int "Gamepad mode at first power-up (0 Xbox 360, 1 DualShock 4, 2 Switch Pro, 3 HID)"
    range 0 3
    default 0
    help
        The mode the board starts in until one is picked on the controller: hold the Stadia
        button with A (Xbox 360), B (DualShock 4), X (Switch Pro) or Y (HID gamepad) for 1.5 s.
        The board remembers the last one picked.

config DONGLE_STATUS_LED_GPIO
    int "Status LED GPIO (-1 for none)"
    range -1 54
    default 3
    help
        The FireBeetle 2 ESP32-P4's own LED is on GPIO3. Fast blink: starting up. Slow blink:
        ready, wake the controller. Steady: the controller is connected.

endmenu
